==Venue==
The [[Zayed Sports City Stadium]] in [[Abu Dhabi]] was announced as the final venue on 11 April 2017.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2017/m=4/news=official-emblem-unveiled-as-uae-prepares-for-kick-off-2879111.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170411221006/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2017/m=4/news=official-emblem-unveiled-as-uae-prepares-for-kick-off-2879111.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 April 2017|title=Official Emblem unveiled as UAE prepares for kick-off|website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|date=11 April 2017}}</ref> The stadium had previously hosted the final in [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup final|2009]] and [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup final|2010]].

==Background==
The FIFA Club World Cup, held annually in December, is contested between the winners of continental club competitions and the winners of the host nation's league.

[[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] qualified for their fourth Club World Cup by winning the [[2016–17 UEFA Champions League]]. The club previously won the [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup|2014]] and [[2016 FIFA Club World Cup|2016]] editions of the Club World Cup, second only to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in number of wins.<ref>{{cite news |date=12 December 2017 |title=Real Madrid target third Club World Cup win |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0240-0e9820a98b4f-4fa8ccf86d53-1000--real-madrid-target-third-club-world-cup-win/ |publisher=UEFA |access-date=13 December 2017}}</ref> Madrid entered the competition in the semi-finals, facing [[UAE Pro-League]] champions [[Al-Jazira Club|Al-Jazira]], who had won against Oceania champion [[Auckland City FC]] of New Zealand and Asian champions [[Urawa Red Diamonds]] of Japan.<ref>{{cite news |last=Hammond |first=Ashley |date=11 December 2017 |title=Almunia: Jazira have fantastic future |url=http://gulfnews.com/sport/football/more-football/almunia-jazira-have-fantastic-future-1.2138898 |work=[[Gulf News]] |access-date=13 December 2017}}</ref> Real Madrid won the match 2–1, after controversial decisions by the referee and [[video assistant referee]] system.<ref>{{cite news |last=Train |first=Rob |date=13 December 2017 |title=Zinedine Zidane to blame for lacklustre Real Madrid in win vs. Al-Jazira |url=http://www.espn.co.uk/football/club/real-madrid/86/blog/post/3309636/real-madrids-lacklustre-ratings-vs-al-jazira-club-world-cup-down-to-zinedine-zidane |publisher=[[ESPN]] |access-date=13 December 2017}}</ref>

Brazilian club [[Grêmio Foot-Ball Porto Alegrense|Grêmio]] qualified for their first Club World Cup by winning the [[2017 Copa Libertadores]] in November. They entered the semi-finals round, facing North American champions [[C.F. Pachuca|Pachuca]] of Mexico, who had defeated African champions [[Wydad Casablanca]] of Morocco in the quarter-finals.<ref>{{cite news |date=9 December 2017 |title=Pachuca beat Wydad Casablanca to reach FIFA Club World Cup semi |url=http://www.espnfc.com.au/report?gameId=499169 |work=[[ESPN FC]] |access-date=13 December 2017}}</ref> Grêmio won the match 1–0 in [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]], on a goal scored by [[Éverton Soares|Éverton]] in the 95th minute.<ref>{{cite news |last=Downie |first=Andrew |date=13 December 2017 |title=FIFA Club World Cup: Gremio reach final after extra time win over Pachuca |url=http://www.smh.com.au/sport/soccer/fifa-club-world-cup-gremio-reach-final-after-extra-time-win-over-pachuca-20171212-h03kjh.html |work=[[Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date=13 December 2017}}</ref>

